<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  ==================================================================
  ORGANISM — the three-lamina body, and the first one with a RETURN PATH

  This is a second body, not a replacement.  `organism.xml` stays at two
  laminae and stays the default: its 1800-value baseline is frozen, and
  a body is added here by dropping a file in rather than by editing the
  one everything else is measured against.  `available_organisms()`
  globs `organism*.xml`, so nothing in code changes to offer this.

  ==================================================================
  WHY IT EXISTS

  The backward pass has been buildable since the gate channels landed
  and unusable on the shipped body, for a reason that is arithmetic
  rather than oversight: circulation's face `output_state` holds ONE
  attribute (CO), and a composite's state value is the weighted mean of
  its attributes — so with one attribute the state IS that attribute
  whatever its weight, and every learning step is exactly zero.  Rule 14
  refuses that rather than letting a return path run forever and move
  nothing.

  Respiration's `transport_state` is the opposite case and was already
  eligible before this file existed: four attributes (Hb, SpO2, CaO2,
  DO2) AND a learning rule its own file declares.  All it lacked was
  somebody above it to send to.  `lamina_metabolic` is that somebody.

  ==================================================================
  WHAT THE SECOND SEAM CARRIES, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT

  Read this before adding another multi-attribute face anywhere.

  A channel carries a composite's STATE VALUE — one number in [0,1] —
  and the receiver maps it onto its own declared range.  When the face
  holds ONE attribute that round-trips exactly: `output_state` is CO, so
  `CO_ref` receives a cardiac output.  When the face holds FOUR, the
  state value is a blend, and mapping it onto any single quantity's
  range would be a lie with a decimal point on it.

  Measured here: `transport_state` sits at 0.6663 while respiration's
  own DO2 reads 988.4 mL/min.  Mapped onto DO2's declared range that
  index would arrive as 1715.8 mL/min — wrong by 74%, in a number
  labelled "oxygen delivery".

  So the receiving attribute is `supply_index`, dimensionless, [0,1],
  and it is named for what it is.  The metabolic lamina turns it into a
  consumption ceiling with its OWN declared capacity, which is the right
  place for that conversion: how much oxygen this tissue could use is a
  fact about the tissue, not about the lung.

  The tension is worth stating plainly rather than hiding: Rule 14 needs
  two or more attributes for a face to be teachable, and two or more
  attributes means the wire carries an index rather than a named
  quantity.  A seam is one or the other.  This one is an index.

  ==================================================================
  THE RETURN PATH

      delta = polarity * lambda_gate * E

  E is the metabolic lamina's lactate error in tolerances; lambda is the
  openness the forward pass used this cycle.  Error is payload and
  payload obeys gates.

  polarity="1" and it is NOT the default, so it is argued here.  The
  sign MULTIPLIES the deviation, exactly as on a tag.  The default -1 is
  right when raising the source RAISES the far outcome.  Here the
  coupling runs the other way: more oxygen supply means LESS lactate.
  Lactate above target gives a positive deviation, and the sender has to
  respond by sending MORE — so the sign has to flip.  Getting this
  backwards does not fail loudly; it diverges quietly, which is why the
  derivation is written down.

  ==================================================================
-->
<organism id="bdt_metabolic" version="2.0">

  <name>Three-Lamina Body</name>
  <description>
    Circulation, respiration and tissue metabolism, stacked. The second
    seam carries an oxygen supply index upward and lactate error back
    down — the first return path in this project that runs on real
    laminae rather than on a body built for a test.
  </description>

  <laminae>
    <lamina id="lamina_circulatory" level="1"/>
    <lamina id="lamina_respiratory" level="2"/>
    <lamina id="lamina_metabolic"   level="3"/>
  </laminae>

  <channels>

    <!-- ==========================================================
         Seam 1 — unchanged from organism.xml.

         Copied rather than referenced because a body is a complete
         declaration; a file that inherited half its channels from
         another one would make "what does this body do" a question
         with two files in the answer.
         ========================================================== -->
    <channel id="ch_co_to_resp"
             from="lamina_circulatory:output_state"
             to="lamina_respiratory:CO_ref"
             time_delay="0">

      <description>
        Cardiac output crossing into oxygen delivery. Held shut while
        the pump is unremarkable, because a respiratory model resting
        on its own declared baseline of 5 L/min is not wrong — it is
        only uninformed.
      </description>

      <extract mode="state"/>

      <transmit>
        <auto_gate id="ta_pump_deviation" refractory_cycles="5">
          <description>
            "Unremarkable" measured against what the receiver already
            assumes, and two-sided so that shock counts as remarkable
            too. Numbers as measured for organism.xml.
          </description>
          <threshold aggregate="deviation" reference="0.30"
                     open_above="0.125" close_below="0.09"/>
        </auto_gate>
      </transmit>

      <receive>
        <key_gate id="rk_co" weight="1.0" rate="0.2"
                  on_close="hold" confidence="0.8">
          <description>
            A receptor for cardiac output specifically: `identity`
            matches the LABEL on the channel.
          </description>
          <key metric="identity"/>
        </key_gate>
      </receive>
    </channel>

    <!-- ==========================================================
         Seam 2 — the new one, and the one with a return path.
         ========================================================== -->
    <channel id="ch_supply_to_tissue"
             from="lamina_respiratory:transport_state"
             to="lamina_metabolic:supply_index"
             time_delay="0">

      <description>
        The oxygen supply index respiration presents, and the lactate
        error the tissue sends back. Unlike seam 1 this one is open
        whenever there is any supply at all: there is no valve between
        a lung and a capillary bed, and a gate that pretended otherwise
        would be modelling a mechanism the body does not have.
      </description>

      <extract mode="state"/>

      <transmit>
        <auto_gate id="ta_supply" refractory_cycles="0">
          <description>
            Open whenever respiration is delivering anything. The two
            boundaries rule 4 requires are still both here and still
            ordered — the band is simply placed near the floor, because
            what this gate is really saying is "the lung is doing
            something", and the only state it shuts for is one where
            transport has collapsed entirely.

            refractory_cycles is 0 for the same reason: a refractory
            period exists to stop a gate re-firing on noise, and a gate
            that is meant to sit open has nothing to re-fire on.
          </description>
          <threshold aggregate="state"
                     open_above="0.10" close_below="0.05"/>
        </auto_gate>
      </transmit>

      <receive>
        <key_gate id="rk_supply" weight="1.0" rate="0.2"
                  on_close="hold" confidence="0.9">
          <description>
            rate 0.2 matches seam 1 and for the same reason: how fast to
            believe an arriving number is the receiver's business. The
            tissue eases toward the supply it is offered rather than
            snapping to it, which keeps this loop and respiration's own
            from pulling on each other through an open channel.
          </description>
          <key metric="identity"/>
        </key_gate>
      </receive>

      <!-- polarity="1": more supply means LESS lactate, so the sign
           flips from the corrective default. See the header. -->
      <backprop from="target_lactate" polarity="1">
        <description>
          Lactate is the outcome a tissue cannot fix by itself. It has
          no pump and no lung; every correction available to it lives
          one lamina down. That is exactly the case a return path is
          for, and it is why this seam carries one while seam 1 does
          not — respiration CAN act on a cardiac output it dislikes,
          by changing how it breathes.
        </description>
      </backprop>
    </channel>

  </channels>

</organism>
